<section>
  <h2>Why not flashcards?</h2>
  <ul class="features">
    <li><strong>Definition cards overfit.</strong> The prompt never changes, so you end up recognizing the definition's phrasing instead of commanding the word. Lexi varies the surface every repetition and keeps the schedule unified.</li>
    <li><strong>Self-grading is decision fatigue.</strong> No Again/Hard/Good/Easy: your solve speed — normalized for clue and word length — raises or lowers each word's ease automatically.</li>
    <li><strong>Cold first reps are brutal.</strong> Flashcards quiz you on words you've never seen. In Lexi, every first encounter starts with the word's illustrated card: image, definition, pictured example sentence, connotation tags.</li>
    <li><strong>Clue quality can't be improvised.</strong> Each clue is produced by a creator → validator → guesser → reviser pipeline, up to seven revisions before acceptance — and keeps improving through in-app feedback.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Everything you've learned hangs overhead</h2>
  <div class="clue-demo">
    <video class="phone-shot" src="/img/demo-sky.mp4" width="600" height="1236"
           autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"
           aria-label="Lexi's home sky zooming from the rocket into a constellation linking transient, transitory, ephemeral, and fugacious — each star carrying its word's art, colored by how soon the word is due"></video>
    <div>
      <p>The home screen is a sky. Every learned word is a star, its color
      tracking the schedule — cool while safely known, warm as a review comes
      due. Words you've trained apart are drawn as constellations, and the
      rocket launches your next puzzle.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Confusable words get trained apart</h2>
  <div class="clue-demo">
    <video class="phone-shot" src="/img/demo-drill.mp4" width="600" height="1236"
           autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"
           aria-label="A Lexi match drill being solved: dale, glen, dell, and dingle dragged onto four illustrated valley scenes, correct matches ringing green and a miss ringing red"></video>
    <div>
      <p>Knowing <em>proclivity</em> isn't much use if you can't say how it
      differs from <em>propensity</em>. Nuance cards state the difference
      precisely, match drills pit whole clusters — <em>dale, glen, dell,
      dingle</em> — against illustrated scenes, and typing a word's confusable
      cousin surfaces the distinction at the moment of the mix-up. Lexi
      Constella, keeper of your word-sky, teaches on the spot.</p>
      <p>Contrastive training and the full companion wardrobe are
      <a href="/premium/">Premium</a>; the puzzles, the reviews, and your sky
      are free.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What's inside</h2>
  <ul class="features">
    <li><strong>20,000 words</strong> ordered roughly by rarity — maximal real-world utility per unit time spent</li>
    <li><strong>Synonym nuance:</strong> tap any two synonyms to read precisely how they differ</li>
    <li><strong>Collections that steer generation:</strong> save words, and puzzles prioritize words with related meanings</li>
    <li><strong>Multiplayer, leaderboards, achievements</strong>, an activity heat map — and streaks you can <em>rekindle</em> after a slip, so one bad week doesn't erase months</li>
    <li><strong>Companions you earn by learning:</strong> nine animals styled by adjectives from the word bank, each unlocked by answering its word</li>
    <li><strong>The Observatory:</strong> a star log for every word, community posts, and study partners near your level</li>
    <li><strong>Reminders fired from words actually due</strong>, not a fixed daily nag</li>
    <li><strong>Profiles</strong> under one iCloud account — separate progress for you and your kid on the same iPad</li>
  </ul>
</section>

